│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 inlineCode "ruff check"
├─3   paragraph[2]
│     ├─0 inlineCode "ruff check"
│     └─1 text " is the primary entrypoint to the Ruff linter. It accepts a list of files or\ndirectories, and lints all discovered Python files, optionally fixing any fixable errors.\nWhen linting a directory, Ruff searches for Python files recursively in that directory\nand all its subdirectories:"
├─4   code "$ ruff check                  # Lint files in the current directory.\n$ ruff check --fix            # Lint files in the current directory and fix any fixable errors.\n$ ruff check --watch          # Lint files in the current directory and re-lint on change.\n$ ruff check path/to/code/    # Lint files in `path/to/code`."
│       lang: "console"
│       meta: null
├─5   paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "For the full list of supported options, run "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "ruff check --help"
│     └─2 text "."
├─6   heading[1]
│     │ depth: 2
│     └─0 text "Rule selection"
├─7   paragraph[7]
│     ├─0 text "The set of enabled rules is controlled via the "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_select"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.select"
│     ├─2 text ",\n"
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_extend-select"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.extend-select"
│     ├─4 text ", and "
│     ├─5 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_ignore"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.ignore"
│     └─6 text " settings."
├─8   paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "Ruff's linter mirrors Flake8's rule code system, in which each rule code consists of a one-to-three\nletter prefix, followed by three digits (e.g., "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "F401"
│     ├─2 text "). The prefix indicates that \"source\" of the rule\n(e.g., "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "F"
│     ├─4 text " for Pyflakes, "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "E"
│     ├─6 text " for pycodestyle, "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "ANN"
│     └─8 text " for flake8-annotations)."
├─9   paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "Rule selectors like "
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_select"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.select"
│     ├─2 text " and "
│     ├─3 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_ignore"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.ignore"
│     ├─4 text " accept either\na full rule code (e.g., "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "F401"
│     ├─6 text ") or any valid prefix (e.g., "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "F"
│     └─8 text "). For example, given the following\nconfiguration file:"
├─10  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─11  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\"E\", \"F\"]\nignore = [\"F401\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─12  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"ruff.toml\""
├─13  code "```toml\n[lint]\nselect = [\"E\", \"F\"]\nignore = [\"F401\"]\n```"
│       lang: null
│       meta: null
├─14  paragraph[11]
│     ├─0  text "Ruff would enable all rules with the "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "E"
│     ├─2  text " (pycodestyle) or "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "F"
│     ├─4  text " (Pyflakes) prefix, with the exception\nof "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "F401"
│     ├─6  text ". For more on configuring Ruff via "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─8  text ", see "
│     ├─9  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "configuration.md"
│     │    └─0 emphasis[1]
│     │        └─0 text "Configuring Ruff"
│     └─10 text "."
├─15  paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "As a special-case, Ruff also supports the "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "ALL"
│     ├─2 text " code, which enables all rules. Note that some\npydocstyle rules conflict (e.g., "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "D203"
│     ├─4 text " and "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "D211"
│     ├─6 text ") as they represent alternative docstring\nformats. Ruff will automatically disable any conflicting rules when "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "ALL"
│     └─8 text " is enabled."
├─16  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "If you're wondering how to configure Ruff, here are some "
│     ├─1 strong[1]
│     │   └─0 text "recommended guidelines"
│     └─2 text ":"
├─17  list[3]
│     │ ordered: false
│     │ start: null
│     │ spread: false
│     ├─0 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[5]
│     │       ├─0 text "Prefer "
│     │       ├─1 link[1]
│     │       │   │ title: null
│     │       │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_select"
│     │       │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.select"
│     │       ├─2 text " over "
│     │       ├─3 link[1]
│     │       │   │ title: null
│     │       │   │ url: "settings.md#lint_extend-select"
│     │       │   └─0 inlineCode "lint.extend-select"
│     │       └─4 text " to make your rule set explicit."
│     ├─1 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[5]
│     │       ├─0 text "Use "
│     │       ├─1 inlineCode "ALL"
│     │       ├─2 text " with discretion. Enabling "
│     │       ├─3 inlineCode "ALL"
│     │       └─4 text " will implicitly enable new rules whenever you upgrade."
│     └─2 listItem[1]
│         │ spread: false
│         │ checked: null
│         └─0 paragraph[5]
│             ├─0 text "Start with a small set of rules ("
│             ├─1 inlineCode "select = [\"E\", \"F\"]"
│             ├─2 text ") and add a group at-a-time. For example,\nyou might consider expanding to "
│             ├─3 inlineCode "select = [\"E\", \"F\", \"B\"]"
│             └─4 text " to enable the popular flake8-bugbear\nextension."
├─18  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, a configuration that enables some of the most popular rules (without being too\npedantic) might look like the following:"
├─19  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "=== \"pyproject.toml\""
├─20  code "```toml\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\n    # pycodestyle\n    \"E\",\n    # Pyflakes\n    \"F\",\n    # pyupgrade\n    \"UP\",\n    # flake8-bugbear\n    \"B\",\n    # flake8-simplify\n    \"SIM\",\n    # isort\n    \"I\",\n]\n```"
